### 3. Application Properties

Add these precise settings to your `application.properties` (or `application-prod.properties`). Adjust the usernames, passwords, and URLs to match your actual server environments.

**File:** `src/main/resources/application.properties`

```properties
# ==========================================
# 1. PRIMARY DATABASE: ORACLE (High Traffic)
# ==========================================
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:oracle:thin:@//your-oracle-db-host:1521/your_service
spring.datasource.username=your_oracle_user
spring.datasource.password=your_oracle_password
spring.datasource.driver-class-name=oracle.jdbc.OracleDriver

# --- Oracle Hikari Connection Pool Settings ---
spring.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size=50
spring.datasource.hikari.minimum-idle=10
spring.datasource.hikari.connection-timeout=30000
spring.datasource.hikari.idle-timeout=600000
spring.datasource.hikari.max-lifetime=1800000


# ==========================================
# 2. SECONDARY DATABASE: HIVE (Background Sync)
# ==========================================
spring.hive.datasource.url=jdbc:hive2://your-hive-server:10000/default
spring.hive.datasource.username=your_hive_user
spring.hive.datasource.password=your_hive_password
spring.hive.datasource.driver-class-name=org.apache.hive.jdbc.HiveDriver

# --- Hive Hikari Connection Pool Settings ---
# Kept small to prevent overloading the Hadoop cluster
spring.hive.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size=10
spring.hive.datasource.hikari.minimum-idle=2
spring.hive.datasource.hikari.connection-timeout=60000
spring.hive.datasource.hikari.idle-timeout=600000
spring.hive.datasource.hikari.max-lifetime=1800000

```
